## 6. Development Roadmap

### 6.1 Phase 0 — Literature & Novelty Audit (Weeks 1–3)

- **Objectives**: Establish exactly what has and hasn't been done in TGNN-based conjunction prediction and MARL-based satellite coordination; identify the specific, defensible novelty claim.
- **Deliverables**: A literature review document (`docs/research-notes/`) with a comparison table of at least 10–15 relevant papers, and a one-paragraph novelty statement reviewed by the mentor.
- **Dependencies**: None — this is the true starting point, before any code.
- **Expected output**: A go/no-go decision on the core research framing, and an explicit list of what will be simplified relative to the original brief (e.g., "we will support N≤50 satellites in simulation, not 'thousands', for the prototype, with a discussion of scaling in the paper").
- **Completion criteria**: Mentor sign-off that the novelty claim is defensible and non-trivial.
- **Possible challenges**: Discovering that a very similar system already exists (e.g., recent ESA/academic TGNN-conjunction papers) — if so, the differentiator must be found immediately, not after months of engineering.

### 6.2 Phase 1 — Data & Simulation Foundation (Weeks 4–9)

- Data ingestion pipeline; propagation correctness validated against known reference orbits.
- Dynamic graph construction with a first pass at node/edge features.
- Non-learned baseline conjunction screener (pairwise distance/threshold-based) — this is the number every learned model must beat.

### 6.3 Phase 2 — TGNN Forecaster (Weeks 10–18)

- Baseline static-GNN conjunction classifier first (simpler, establishes the graph-learning pipeline works at all).
- Extend to temporal architecture (e.g., TGN, EvolveGCN, or a custom temporal-attention layer over graph snapshots — **[ASSUMPTION]**: exact architecture choice deferred to Phase 0/1 findings).
- Calibration and evaluation against the Phase 1 baseline.

### 6.4 Phase 3 — MARL Coordination (Weeks 19–28)

- Single-agent RL sanity check first (one satellite, avoid one conjunction) before multi-agent.
- Multi-agent training (MAPPO as the default starting algorithm — well-supported, comparatively stable to train relative to QMIX/MADDPG for continuous-ish action spaces).
- Reward shaping iteration — this will take longer than planned; budget for it explicitly.

### 6.5 Phase 4 — Integration & Physics-Constrained Optimization (Weeks 29–33)

- Connect TGNN output → MARL input.
- Add hard feasibility constraints (fuel budget, maneuver limits) as a constrained-optimization or action-masking layer.

### 6.6 Phase 5 — Evaluation, Ablation, and Paper Writing (Weeks 34–40)

- Full ablation suite (see Section 14).
- Draft paper in parallel with final experiments, not after.

*(This maps to the Weekly and Monthly plans in Sections 17–18; treat those as the operational detail behind this roadmap.)*

