## 12. Dataset Guide

| Aspect | Detail |
|---|---|
| Primary source | [Space-Track.org](https://www.space-track.org) (requires free registration) for authoritative TLE/catalog data; [CelesTrak](https://celestrak.org) for convenient public mirrors of common TLE sets |
| Download process | Scripted retrieval via `datasets/scripts/`, never manual downloads, so every dataset snapshot is reproducible |
| Directory placement | Raw snapshots → `datasets/raw/<source>/<date>/`; cleaned/derived → `datasets/processed/` |
| Preprocessing | Deduplicate NORAD IDs, filter to relevant orbital regime (e.g., LEO only for the prototype), validate TLE checksum |
| Cleaning | Drop malformed/expired TLEs; flag objects with propagation errors above a defined threshold |
| Validation | Automated schema + sanity checks (epoch within expected range, orbital elements physically plausible) run as part of the ingestion pipeline, not manually |
| Versioning | DVC-tracked or explicitly dated snapshot folders — every experiment config references an exact dataset version |
| Expected size | A LEO-focused subset is on the order of several thousand objects per snapshot; exact figure depends on final scope decided in Phase 0 |

---

## 13. Model Training Pipeline

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    A[Processed graph snapshots] --> B[Feature engineering\norbital elements, derived proximity features]
    B --> C[Train/val/test split\nby time, not randomly]
    C --> D[TGNN training loop]
    D --> E[Checkpoint saving\nversioned per experiment run]
    E --> F[Evaluation on held-out future epochs]
    F --> G[Metrics + calibration report]
```

- **Input**: sequences of graph snapshots with node features (orbital elements, object type) and edge features (relative distance, relative velocity, time-to-closest-approach).
- **Processing**: normalization of physical quantities (critical — raw orbital element scales vary by orders of magnitude); temporal windowing.
- **Feature engineering**: must be justified physically, not just empirically — e.g., relative velocity direction matters for collision geometry, not just magnitude.
- **Training**: time-based train/val/test split is mandatory — a random split would leak future information into training, invalidating the forecasting claim entirely.
- **Validation**: held-out future time windows only.
- **Checkpoint saving**: every checkpoint tagged with exact config + data version.
- **Evaluation**: precision/recall on conjunction *events* (not raw timesteps), calibration of predicted probabilities, and forecasting-horizon degradation curves (how much does accuracy drop at day+1 vs day+5?).

---

## 14. Testing Strategy

| Test type | Scope | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Unit tests | Individual functions | SGP4 wrapper matches reference ephemeris within tolerance |
| Integration tests | Module-to-module interfaces | Graph constructor correctly consumes propagation layer output |
| End-to-end tests | Full pipeline on a small fixed case | 3-satellite synthetic scenario runs ingestion → forecast → coordination → maneuver plan without error |
| Performance tests | Scaling behavior | Graph construction and TGNN inference time vs. number of satellites (10, 100, 1000) |
| Stress tests | Edge cases | Object re-entry, TLE gaps, extremely close conjunctions, malformed input data |
| Validation datasets | Known ground truth | Historical, publicly documented close-approach or conjunction events used as sanity checks |

**Ablation studies** (for the paper, not just engineering QA):
- TGNN vs. static-GNN vs. non-learned baseline (isolates value of temporal modeling).
- MARL coordinated policy vs. independent single-agent policies per satellite (isolates value of coordination).
- With vs. without physics-constrained optimization layer (isolates value of the feasibility layer).

**Sensitivity & scalability analysis**: performance vs. constellation size; performance vs. forecasting horizon; robustness to injected TLE noise.

---

## 15. Experiment Tracking

- **Folder organization**: one config per experiment under `experiments/configs/`; results linked by experiment ID, not by memory.
- **Naming convention**: `<phase>_<model>_<variant>_<date>` (e.g., `phase2_tgnn_temporal-attn_2026-09-01`).
- **Model versioning**: every checkpoint tagged with git commit hash + dataset version + config hash.
- **Metrics logging**: via W&B/MLflow — loss curves, calibration, precision/recall, delta-v cost, per-run.
- **Result comparison**: dashboards comparing all runs within a phase side-by-side before any figure is finalized for the paper.
- **Ablation studies**: tracked as their own experiment group, not mixed in with hyperparameter-tuning runs.

---

## 16. Documentation Strategy

- **API documentation**: auto-generated from docstrings (e.g., via `mkdocs` + `mkdocstrings`) if/when an API layer is built.
- **Architecture documentation**: Architecture Decision Records (ADRs) under `docs/architecture/` — one short markdown file per significant decision (e.g., "why MAPPO over QMIX"), including the alternatives considered and why rejected.
- **Research notes**: weekly literature/experiment notes under `docs/research-notes/`, dated.
- **Meeting notes**: every mentor checkpoint logged under `docs/meeting-notes/` with decisions and action items, not just discussion summary.
- **Experiment logs**: linked from `experiments/results/` back to the relevant research note.
- **Decision logs**: any deviation from this README's plan (e.g., scope cuts) logged as an ADR, not silently absorbed.

---

## 17. Weekly Development Plan

> Populated in detail through Phase 0–1 (Weeks 1–9); later weeks will be refined as earlier phases complete — committing to detailed weekly tasks for Week 30 today would mean inventing plans that current unknowns (Phase 0 findings) will certainly change.

| Week | Objective | Key tasks | Expected output | Risks |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | Literature review kickoff | Divide ~15 papers across 4 members; each summarizes 3–4 | Shared annotated bibliography | Team may find the exact idea already published — treat as a finding, not a failure |
| 2 | Literature synthesis | Group discussion; identify gaps and candidate novelty angles | Draft novelty statement (v1) | Premature convergence on a novelty claim without enough papers reviewed |
| 3 | Novelty audit sign-off | Mentor review of novelty statement; scope decisions (e.g., max constellation size for prototype) | Signed-off novelty statement + explicit scope document | Scope too ambitious relative to 8-10 month timeline |
| 4 | Repo & CI bootstrap | Set up repo structure, pre-commit, CI skeleton, empty test suite passing | Green CI on trivial commit | Tooling setup eats more time than planned — timebox it |
| 5 | Data ingestion v1 | TLE fetch script + raw storage + schema validation | Reproducible raw dataset snapshot | Space-Track rate limits / access approval delays |
| 6 | Propagation validation | SGP4 wrapper + validation against reference ephemeris | Propagation unit tests passing with quantified accuracy | Coordinate frame bugs (a classic, easy-to-miss source of silent errors) |
| 7 | Graph construction v1 | Define node/edge features; build snapshot graphs | Graph objects for a small fixed satellite set, tested | Feature choices not yet physically justified — flag for Phase 2 revisit |
| 8 | Baseline conjunction screener | Non-learned pairwise threshold baseline | Baseline precision/recall numbers on validation data | Baseline may be stronger than expected, raising the bar for the TGNN |
| 9 | Phase 1 wrap-up & review | Mentor review of Phase 1 deliverables | Go/no-go into Phase 2 | — |

*(Weeks 10+ to be added at the start of Phase 2, following this same table format.)*

