## 21. Contributor Guide

**For a new contributor joining mid-project:**

1. Read this README fully before opening any code.
2. Read the ADRs under `docs/architecture/` to understand *why* current decisions were made, not just what they are.
3. Set up your environment per Section 11.
4. Run the full test suite; it should pass on a clean checkout — if it doesn't, that's a bug worth flagging immediately, not working around.
5. Pick up an issue tagged `good-first-issue` (or ask the module owner for a small, well-scoped starting task).
6. Open a draft PR early, even before the work is complete, to get early feedback on direction.

**Coding expectations**: match existing module style; add tests for new functionality (see Section 14); no PR merges without at least one review.

**Documentation expectations**: any new module gets a `README.md`; any non-obvious design decision gets an ADR.

---

## 22. Troubleshooting

| Issue | Likely cause | Solution |
|---|---|---|
| SGP4 propagation gives wildly wrong positions | Wrong epoch reference frame or units mismatch (degrees vs radians) | Check units at every function boundary; add explicit unit assertions |
| CI fails only on GitHub Actions, not locally | Environment/dependency version mismatch | Pin exact versions in `requirements.txt`; match CI Python version to local |
| TGNN training loss doesn't decrease | Unnormalized input features (common with raw orbital elements) | Verify feature normalization; check for NaNs from degenerate orbits |
| MARL policy converges to trivial "never maneuver" behavior | Reward not penalizing missed avoidance enough, or fuel-cost penalty dominating | Revisit reward shaping (see Section 6.4); this is a very common early MARL failure mode, not a bug in the framework |
| Dataset download fails intermittently | Space-Track/CelesTrak rate limiting | Add retry/backoff logic; cache raw downloads locally |
| Experiment results not reproducible | Missing config/data version pinning | Always log commit hash + data version + config hash with every run (Section 15) |

*(Expand this table continuously as real issues are hit — a troubleshooting section written in advance of actually building the system can only list generic, predictable issues, which is exactly what's shown above; real ones should be added as encountered.)*

---

## 23. FAQ

**Q: Why not just use an existing conjunction-screening tool and focus only on the RL coordination piece?**
A: That's a legitimate scope-reduction option and should be seriously considered at Phase 0 if the TGNN forecasting piece turns out not to add a defensible novel contribution on its own — better to cut scope deliberately than dilute effort across two under-baked contributions.

**Q: Why start with SGP4 instead of higher-fidelity propagation?**
A: Speed and universality — SGP4 is what's compatible with public TLE data at scale. The accuracy limitation is a known, citable trade-off, not a hidden flaw, and should be stated explicitly in any resulting paper.

**Q: What if our novelty claim turns out to already exist in the literature?**
A: That is the entire purpose of Phase 0 — better to find this in week 2 than month 6. It is a normal outcome of a literature review, not a project failure, and the response is to refine the differentiator, not abandon the project.

**Q: Do we need real operator collaboration to publish?**
A: No — public TLE-based prototypes with clearly stated limitations are a normal, accepted basis for a workshop/conference submission at this level.

**Q: What if the 8–10 month timeline is too short for the full scope described in Section 1?**
A: Very likely, honestly — the roadmap in Section 6 already assumes some scope reduction from the original full vision (see the Phase 0 explicit scope document requirement). This should be revisited candidly at each phase boundary rather than assumed away.

