## 24. Appendix

### Useful commands
```bash
pytest tests/unit -v                     # run unit tests verbosely
pytest tests/integration                 # run integration tests
pre-commit run --all-files               # lint/format check
python -m backend.simulation.run --help  # simulation CLI help
```

### Folder templates
See Section 4 for the canonical structure; copy the `backend/models/tgnn/` layout as a template for any new model module (`__init__.py`, `model.py`, `train.py`, `evaluate.py`, `README.md`).

### Naming conventions
- Python: `snake_case` for functions/variables, `PascalCase` for classes.
- Experiment configs: `<phase>_<model>_<variant>_<date>.yaml`.
- Branches/commits: see Section 9.

### Glossary
| Term | Meaning |
|---|---|
| TLE | Two-Line Element set — the standard compact representation of an orbit used with SGP4 |
| SGP4 | Simplified General Perturbations model 4 — standard fast orbital propagator for TLE data |
| Conjunction | A close approach between two objects in orbit |
| TGNN | Temporal Graph Neural Network — a GNN architecture that models graphs evolving over time |
| MARL | Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning |
| MAPPO | Multi-Agent Proximal Policy Optimization |
| Delta-v | Change in velocity required for a maneuver — the standard currency of fuel cost in astrodynamics |
| J2 perturbation | The dominant orbital perturbation caused by Earth's oblateness |

### Research terminology
- **Ablation study**: an experiment that removes/isolates one component to measure its individual contribution.
- **Calibration**: how well a model's predicted probabilities match observed frequencies.
- **Held-out future epoch**: a time-based (not random) validation split, required for any forecasting claim to be valid.

---

## 25. Closing Recommendations (from the Mentor role)

Per the brief's own rule 11, here are direct, unsoftened recommendations rather than a purely descriptive close:

1. **Do not skip Phase 0.** The single biggest risk to this project is discovering in month 5 that the core novelty claim doesn't hold up. Spend real weeks 1–3 on literature, not a token review.
2. **Scope down explicitly, in writing.** "Thousands of satellites" and full multi-operator coordination are very unlikely to be achievable at prototype fidelity in 8–10 months with 4 undergraduate contributors. State the actual prototype scale (tens to low hundreds of simulated satellites is a defensible, honest target) in the Phase 0 scope document, and frame the larger vision as future work — reviewers respect honest scoping far more than an overclaimed abstract that the results don't support.
3. **Build the non-learned baseline before any learned model.** Every claimed improvement is meaningless without a credible baseline number to beat, and validating the baseline forces the team to get the physics/data pipeline right first, when it's cheapest to fix.
4. **Treat reward design in Section 6.4 as its own multi-week research task**, not an implementation afterthought — reward mis-specification is the most common reason MARL projects silently fail to produce meaningful coordination behavior.
5. **Keep a written decision log (ADRs) from week 1.** Reviewers and the mentor will ask "why did you choose X over Y" at every stage — having that answer already written down, made in real time rather than reconstructed after the fact, will materially strengthen both code reviews and the eventual paper's related-work/design-justification sections.
6. **Revisit this README itself at every phase boundary.** Several sections here are explicitly marked [ASSUMPTION] or left as skeletons because concrete decisions don't exist yet — this document should evolve with the project, not remain frozen as a Week-1 artifact that stops matching reality by Month 3.
